\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA continues buying HK dollars in offshore market.   \b0\par\par\uc2 220 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 02:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, March 17 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA), the territory's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reported it bought HK$390 million from banks in exchange for U.S. dollars in offshore trade overnight to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed the purchase of HK$148 million during \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading hours late on Wednesday. On Tuesday, it had bought HK$780 million in London trade.   \par \par \uc2 Under Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, the HKMA is committed to defending the pegged rate of HK$7.8 by buying Hong Kong dollars in exchange for U.S. dollars.   \par \par \uc2 Real time information available on Reuters page showed the transaction.   \par \par \uc2 At 0136 GMT, the Hong Kong dollar was trading at 7.7996/97 per U.S. dollar versus 7.7998/99 in late Asian trade on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 The latest move by the HKMA will reduce the aggregate balance - the sum of balances on clearing accounts maintained by banks with the HKMA - to HK$4.358 billion after settlement on Friday, its lowest level in five months, from HK$15.789 billion at the beginning of the year.   \par \par \uc2 A recent decline in the aggregate balance in the banking system and rising interbank rates have increased pressure on local banks to raise interest rates.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050317e13h001mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
