\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-HKMA  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to support HK dollar.   \b0\par\par\uc2 507 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 12:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 , March 9 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) stepped in four times on Wednesday afternoon to support the Hong Kong dollar amid accelerating arbitrage trade on speculation that interest rates will rise soon.   \par \par \uc2 "People are seeing that low interest rates in Hong Kong are in danger of disappearing and they want to take advantage of them while they can," said Tim Condon, chief economist at ING Financial Markets. "That's the arbitrage trade."   \par \par \uc2 Under Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, the HKMA is committed to defending the pegged rate of HK$7.8 by buying Hong Kong dollars in exchange for U.S. dollars at that level.   \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday afternoon the dollar hit 7.8 several times and the HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  four times within half an hour, soaking up HK$2.148 billion in exchange for U.S. dollars.   \par \par \uc2 By 1041 GMT, the Hong Kong dollar was trading at 7.998/99 per U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Speculation that Hong Kong banks might raise rates ahead of a possible U.S. rate rise when the Federal Reserve meets on March 22 heightened on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Raymond Or, general manager of HSBC, the territory's biggest bank, indicated that rates could go up soon if the overnight interest rate remained at current levels. However, HSBC Chairman David Eldon said pressure for an early rate hike was not significant.   \par \par \uc2 Ample liquidity in the Hong Kong banking system has enabled Hong Kong to ignore a recent upturn in U.S. rates, giving investors the opportunity to earn an interest rate pick up they sell Hong Kong dollars for U.S. dollars.   \par \par \uc2 However, fund flows to the territory have been easing in recent months, on expectations that interest rates will have to follow U.S. rate moves sooner or later and on declining demand for the Hong Kong dollar as a proxy to a revaluation of China's yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Though liquidity is still ample, the aggregate balance has fallen to around HK$8 billion from more than HK$15 billion in late January, raising the prospect that banks will have to raise rates.   \par \par \uc2 "I think banks may raise interest rates this week as the aggregate balance has been reduced quite a lot in recent months," a dealer at a European bank said.   \par \par \uc2 Once Wednesday's outflows are settled on Friday, the aggregate balance will fall to HK$5.541 billion. However, that is still well above the normal average of between HK$500 million-HK$1 billion.   \par \par \uc2 Speculation that China would soon allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate slightly - which triggered heavy investment in Hong Kong-listed Chinese companies last year - has eased as recent comments by Chinese officials suggested an imminent revaluation of the yuan was unlikely.   \par \par \uc2 China's Finance Minister Jin Renqing told a news conference on Wednesday that the yuan's exchange rate was appropriate.   \par \par \uc2 "The renminbi (yuan) exchange rate conforms with China's economic development and our foreign trade situation and should be kept basically stable," Jin said.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050309e13900710\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
