\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED - CORRECTED-HKMA continues buying HK dollars for fourth day.  \b0\par\par\uc2 295 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2005\par 03:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 In HONG KONG story headlined "HKMA continues buying HK dollars for the fourth day" please read in first paragraph "reported it bought HK$390 million" instead of "HK$390 billion" (correcting billion to million).  \par \par \uc2 A corrected story follows.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Feb 4 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA), the territory's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reported it bought HK$390 million from banks in exchange for U.S. dollars in New York trade overnight to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Since Monday, the HKMA has bought a total of HK$6.536 billion, including Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Under \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, the HKMA is committed to defending the pegged rate of HK$7.8 by buying Hong Kong dollars in exchange for U.S. dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Real time information available on Reuters page showed the transaction.  \par \par \uc2 At 0935 GMT, the Hong Kong dollar was trading at 7.7994/95 per U.S. dollar versus 7.7997/98 in late Asian trade on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has suffered selling pressure recently because U.S. interest rates are higher than Hong Kong rates, enabling traders to earn an interest rate spread if they sell Hong Kong dollars for U.S. dollars.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Hong Kong banks decided to keep interest rates unchanged despite another 25 basis point increase in U.S. interest rates earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 A further widening in the interest rate gap between the Hong Kong dollar and the U.S. dollar could trigger further fund outflows, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The latest move by the HKMA will reduce the aggregate balance - the sum of balances on clearing accounts maintained by banks with the HKMA - to HK$9.265 billion after settlement on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050204e124002jw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
