\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar off vs Asia on persistent China speculation.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2004\par 19:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Oct 8 (Reuters) - The dollar tumbled across the board following a below-consensus U.S. jobs report on Friday, but the heaviest selling was against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , amid mounting speculation that China could soon relax its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar posted its biggest one-day loss in over a year against the yen; and despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar surged to its highest level since the end of February.  \par \par \uc2 "The euro is not the focus: it's Asia," said a senior trader at a large U.S. bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit multi-month lows against several rivals on Friday - and in the case of the Canadian dollar, a new 11-year low - after the Labor Department said 96,000 new jobs were created last month. Economists had expected 148,000 new jobs.  \par \par \uc2 But the gains in most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are being eclipsed by the sharp upticks in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Around 1:00 p.m. EDT (1700 GMT) in New York, the dollar was trading around three-week lows of 109.39 yen , marking the biggest single-day drop since Sept. 22 last year, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 And the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA), the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , selling HK$1.557 billion for U.S. dollars on Friday to maintain the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 But "it doesn't seem to have done much good," the senior trader said, noting that the Hong Kong dollar was trading at HK$7.7819 per dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged at around HK$7.80 per dollar. Forward contracts also showed increased bets on some sort of upward revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 A major factor at play here is China and the mounting speculation that Beijing may soon loosen the peg keeping the yuan around 8.28 per dollar. Analysts generally expect any moves along these lines would strengthen the yuan and lead to Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation across the board.  \par \par \uc2 Chinese officials told the Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers meeting in Washington last weekend that they remain on a path toward adopting a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. But they also repeated they will proceed at their own pace and gave no timetable.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation, however, shows no sign of dissipating. A Korean newspaper reported China could revalue the yuan as soon as next week. And the White House confirmed on Thursday that \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  was raised in President George W. Bush's telephone conversation with Chinese President Hu Jintao that day.  \par \par \uc2 "Speculation is building of a China revaluation," said a dealer at a Japanese bank in New York. This and the payrolls data were the two "triggers" that shot down the dollar on Friday, he added.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041008e0a800el2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
