\par\par \b \uc2 HK dollar firms in wake of HKMA support.   \b0\par\par\uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2004\par 11:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     HONG KONG, Sept 2 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong dollar\par strengthened above its peg against the U.S. unit on Thursday, in\par the wake of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par (HKMA) to support it in the previous session.\par     At 0829 GMT the Hong Kong dollar exchange rate        was\par trading at 7.7996/97 per U.S. dollar, firmer than 7.7999/7.80\par late on Wednesday.\par     The HKMA, the territory's de facto \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , bought a\par total of HK$2.73 billion from banks during onshore and offshore\par trading hours on Wednesday.\par     Under Hong Kong's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board system, the HKMA is\par committed to defending the pegged rate of HK$7.80 by buying Hong\par Kong dollars in exchange for U.S. dollars.\par     Dealers said there was some profit taking on the U.S. dollar\par in the wake of the HKMA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But they expected the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong\par Kong\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar's bounce to be short-lived as investors would\par continue to take advantage of comparatively higher U.S. interest\par rates.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "As long as Hong Kong dollar interbank interest rates remain\par below U.S. dollar rates, this provides an arbitrage trading\par opportunity," one trader said.\par     The local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has suffered selling pressure recently,\par amid heavy fund outflows triggered by arbitrage plays as Hong\par Kong lags behind the United States in hiking interest rates.\par     Hong Kong banks have not followed the Fed's last two moves\par because the banking system is awash with funds and on concerns\par that higher rates could hobble weak loan growth in the city.\par     Strong capital inflows to Hong Kong late last year boosted\par bank liquidity. But the aggregate balance -- the sum of balances\par on clearing accounts maintained by banks with the HKMA -- has\par been declining in recent months, from a peak of HK$54.665 billion\par at the end of April to HK$4.338 billion on Friday.\par     "Only when the aggregate balance falls back to its normal\par level, which is around HK$400-500 million, will we see Hong Kong\par dollar interbank interest rates at similar levels to their U.S.\par dollar counterparts," Joseph Yam, chief executive of the HKMA,\par told reporters on Thursday.\par     Some dealers said interest rate rises would have little\par negative impact on Hong Kong's economy, especially given the\par global economic recovery.\par     "A small interest rate rise will likely have more\par psychological than real impact on the economy, as interest rates\par in Hong Kong are now very low," one dealer from a European bank\par said.\par     Hong Kong dollar forwards were rangebound on Thursday.\par Discounts on forwards opened narrower after fund outflows on\par Wednesday but then some selling emerged in the afternoon as\par short-term money rates eased.\par     The discount on one-year forwards          was quoted at\par 445/425 pips by late afternoon versus Wednesday's close of\par 450/430 pips.\par  \par                    Close         Morning       Previous Close\par                    At 0829 GMT   At 0250 GMT   At 0856 GMT\par  HK$ SPOT          7.7996/97     7.7997/98     7.7999/7.80\par  FORWARDS\par  Three-month       -181 / -176   -179 / -174   -188 / -183\par  Six-month         -315 / -305   -310 / -300   -320 / -310\par  One-year          -445 /