\par\par \b \uc2 WEEK AHEAD-HK stocks seen up on high earnings expectation.  \b0\par\par\uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2004\par 06:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, July 25 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks are seen rising this week, boosted by high expectations for earnings from Bank of East Asia, which kicks off the latest reporting season.  \par \par \uc2 But weak U.S. markets, hurt by disappointing earnings and high oil prices, may limit gains as investors eye further volatility on Wall Street.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of East Asia Ltd. (0023.HK), Hong Kong's fifth-largest lender, is expected to post strong interim earnings on Friday due to low provision charges.  \par \par \uc2 The results will also give a taste of earnings ahead from global bank and Hang Seng heavyweight HSBC Holdings Plc. (0005.HK), and its local unit Hang Seng Bank Ltd. (0011.HK).  \par \par \uc2 "Investors are expected to position themselves on hopes that blue chips will post upbeat earnings," said Linus Yip, strategist at First Shanghai Securities Ltd.  \par \par \uc2 A slew of smaller firms will also provide an early peek into the health of regional earnings including operators of the Peninsula hotel chain Hongkong and Shanghai Hotels Ltd (0045.HK), TV maker Skyworth Digital Holdings Ltd (0751.HK) and chip designer Solomon Systech (International) Ltd along with Internet firm TOM Online Inc. .  \par \par \uc2 Traders see the benchmark Hang Seng Index , which is heavily weighted toward banks and property, trading as high as 12,600.  \par \par \uc2 The Hang Seng gained 2.44 percent last week, closing at 12,352.09 on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Property shares, such as Sun Hung Kai Properties (0016.HK) and property-heavy conglomerate Wharf Holdings (0004.HK) may gain further ground.  \par \par \uc2 "Property has recorded strong home sales over the past few weeks," said Lionel Kwok, chief investment officer at BOCI-Prudential Asset Management  \par \par \uc2 Investment bank J.P. Morgan expects office rents in prime business districts to rise by up to 40 percent this year due to strong demand and limited supply.  \par \par \uc2 Investors are also watching property companies that rent heavily sought after retail space such as Causeway Bay landlord Hysan Development Co. Ltd. (0014.HK).  \par \par \uc2 The firm's shares have more than doubled in the past year, but analysts are tempted by Hysan's modern shopping malls amid a retail boom spawned by a flood of mainland Chinese tourists.  \par \par \uc2 WALL STREET WEIGHS  \par \par \uc2 But an earnings-related rally is likely to be weighed down early in the week by a tumbled on Wall Street on Friday after disappointing earnings reports.  \par \par \uc2 More U.S. companies are set to report in the coming week. Investors are also nervously eyeing security concerns during the U.S. Democratic convention in Boston along with persistently high world oil prices which still hover above US$40 a barrel.  \par \par \uc2 The blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average fell for the fifth week in a row last week and closed below the key 10,000 level for the first time in two months.  \par \par \uc2 The tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite Index notched a fresh low for the year on Friday, falling 2.12 percent to 1,849.28.  \par \par \uc2 Renewed signs that international investors are moving funds out of Hong Kong could also limit upside for Hong Kong shares.  \par \par \uc2 The territory's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week was seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, which is pegged to the U.S. unit.  \par \par \uc2 International fund flows are viewed as a key indicator for the Hong Kong market. A flood of money entered the territory late last year thanks to a weak U.S. dollar and high profile China IPO's, but traders said funds had been moving out of the territory in recent weeks due to lacklustre local stocks and arbitrage trades on higher U.S. interest rates.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040725e07p001md\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
