\par\par \b \uc2 HK stocks end lower by midday on profit taking.  \b0\par\par\uc2 554 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2004\par 06:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, July 22 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks sank 0.60 percent by midday on Thursday after a weak performance on Wall Street, where worries over slowing corporate earnings growth rattled investor confidence.   \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Hang Seng Index  ended down 74.61 points at 12,320.50 by midday. Turnover was lighter at HK$5.5 billion (US$705 million) than HK$8.5 billion on Wednesday morning.   \par \par \uc2  Investors also took profits after a sharp 270-point gain during Wednesday's session, fuelled by market-soothing comments from U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan.   \par \par \uc2  "Hong Kong stocks are taking a breather after Wednesday's gain, which I believe is fairly healthy," said Francis Kwok, director at Peace Town Securities Ltd.   \par \par \uc2  All but five of the 33 blue chips ended in the negative territory by midday, led by technology shares after the U.S. tech board tumbled more than two percent.   \par \par \uc2  Ports-to-telecom conglomerate Hutchison Whampoa Ltd. (0013.HK), an index heavyweight, dipped 0.93 percent to HK$53.50. Local newspapers said Japanes electronics giant NEC Corp. is interested in buying stakes in the planned listing of Hutchison's emerging markets telecoms business.   \par \par \uc2  Lenovo Group (0992.HK), China's largest personal computer maker and the top blue-chip loser, fell 3.49 percent to HK$2.075.   \par \par \uc2  Property counters also gave up recent gains. Cheung Kong (Holdings) Ltd. (0001.HK) fell 0.84 percent to HK$59 while Henderson Land Development Co. Ltd. (0012.HK) slipped 0.87 percent to HK$34.   \par \par \uc2  Worries that more funds may leave Hong Kong also curbed investor sentiment.   \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been pressured in recent weeks after the U.S. raised interest rates at the end of June. Hong Kong banks left their rates unchanged, making U.S. dollar denominated investments more attractive.   \par \par \uc2  Market players have speculated funds may leave Hong Kong given the interest rate differential and the prospect that the spread may widen if the U.S. lifts rates further in August.   \par \par \uc2  However, dealers dismissed worries that this could be a prelude of a possible comeback of the 1998 Asia financial crisis. They said there was no evidence of unusually large short stock positions and the outflow of funds from Hong Kong was merely arbitrage trade.   \par \par \uc2  During the Asia financial crisis, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to foil speculators who had simultaneously attacked the Hong Kong dollar and taken short positions in stocks and futures to benefit from falling stock prices.   \par \par \uc2  "Unlike 1998 when financial markets were rattled by weak Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and property market, our economy now is riding high on healthy retail, tourism and real estate sectors. Interest rates are also low," Kwok added.   \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, shares in Hong Kong property developer Far East Consortium International Ltd. (0035.HK) bucked the market downtrend, jumping 7.33 percent to HK$1.61 after the firm said it planned to sell a property in the heart of Kowloon for HK$468.8 million.   \par \par \uc2  Shares of ship chartering and transportation company Jinhui Holdings Co. Ltd. (0137.HK) extended Wednesday's losses, falling 6.47 percent to HK$7.95 after tumbling 24.44 percent in the previous session on a profit warning.   \par \par \uc2  (US$1=HK$7.8).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040722e07m000ur\par }\page {\par\fs2