the local unit at that rate. This essentially puts a floor to the Hong Kong dollar, but theoretically not a ceiling as there is no provision to cap the local unit at a specific level when it strengthens - what is known locally as "strong side undertaking."  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Proxy For China Yuan \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the 1997-1998 regional crisis, falling Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exerted strong pressure on the Hong Kong dollar to do likewise, but the latter stayed supported by the Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the territory's de facto \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . More recently, however, the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  faces a different threat as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  participants use it as a proxy to the China yuan, buying it up whenever speculation mounts the Chinese authorities are ready for an appreciation of the yuan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This was what happened last September when the international pressure gathered after the Group of Seven industrialized nations called for more flexible exchange rates - interpreted as an euphemism for a stronger Chinese yuan, and hence, the Hong Kong dollar. At the same time, the territory faced a deluge of funds anticipating a recovery in Hong Kong's stock and property markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hong Kong dollar suddenly strengthened well beyond its usual narrow range around the HK$7.80 peg rate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  subsequently injected over HK$50 billion into the market to ease the upward pressure on the local unit. Today, much of that money remains in the territory's banking system. The aggregate balance, a measure of interbank liquidity, stood at over HK$35 billion Tuesday, compared with its usual historical level of about HK$1 billion. That large amount of idle funds in the interbank market has kept short-term interest rates near zero percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has tried - unsuccessfully - to cajole banks to deposit the local dollars in U.S. dollars, with the authority's Chief Executive Joseph Yam pointing out that the one full percentage point interest rate earned on overnight funds was no small opportunity cost for large sums - which are what the banks are holding.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But banks remain wary, arguing that the risks stack up against them as long the Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is vague about its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy regarding the upside limit of the Hong Kong dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rush of funds into Hong Kong last year caught most \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  players by surprise. The Hong Kong dollar strengthened to around HK$7.70 to the U.S. dollar within days, forcing many scrambling to cover their short positions with deep losses.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Wary Of Sharp Rise In HK Dollar \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Such unpleasant memory kept players highly wary of the possibility the Hong Kong dollar can rise sharply on big fund inflows," the trader said, adding that "the flow of funds is highly unpredictable."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  did later \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stabilize the market, and it has said it will act as needed to prevent volatility on the strong side of the peg.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But it has stayed ambiguous over what it considers an excessively strong level for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Yam, for instance, has repeatedly said he sees no need to create a strong side undertaking. He argues that kind of commitment would be undesirable because it would encourage speculators to short the Hong Kong dollar if the outlook on the city's economy suddenly turns bearish.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "To fix a level to sell the Hong Kong dollar will enable speculators to calculate their costs and risks (precisely)," Yam told legislators recently.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For banks, that will be enough for them to shy away from the idea of converting their excess interbank funds into U.S. dollars, and short term rates will continue to hover near zero.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For now, the low rates have given Hong Kong's recovering economy an extra kick, helping to encourage domestic consumption and investment.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The city posted a 6.8% year-on-year expansion in gross domestic product in the first quarter of 2004, as domestic consumption picked up, adding to growth in exports and tourism. The worst seems to be over for weak domestic appetite, with the composite consumer price index bottoming in the third quarter of last year. The territory still faces declining prices although the falls are moderating. April CPI registered a 1.5% fall from the year before.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists warn though that the abnormally low rate environment, if it is allowed to continue for a lengthy period, could lead to problems. Asset bubbles may result from excessive speculation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Raymond Tsoi, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; raymond.tsoi@dowjones.com \par    (Raymond Tsoi is a reporter at Dow Jones Newswires' Hong Kong bureau. He has reported on money market for the last two years.) \par    -Edited by Lim Mui Khi \par    \