\par\par \b \uc2 HK stocks seen pressured by weak global markets.   \b0\par\par\uc2 481 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2004\par 03:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, May 18 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks are expected to face further selling pressure on Tuesday, although a technical rebound after recent sharp declines may lift the benchmark index initially.   \par \par \uc2 The blue-chip Hang Seng Index fell 2.74 percent on Monday to 10,967.65.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the index had support at 10,800 although getting back above the 11,000 level would prove difficult given weak global market conditions.   \par \par \uc2 A list of factors have hurt global stock market sentiment including global security fears, high oil prices, anticipation of a U.S. interest rate hike and an overheating Chinese economy.   \par \par \uc2 "All these factors will continue to dominate in the short term," said Ben Kwong, associate director at KGI Asia Ltd.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks stumbled on Monday after the killing of a key figure in Iraq's new government heightened investor worries about global security and helped send oil prices to fresh record highs.   \par \par \uc2 The blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average fell 1.06 percent to 9,907, while the tech heavy Nasdaq Composite Index lost 1.45 percent to 1,877.   \par \par \uc2 Asian markets showed signs of a slight rebound early on Tuesday after a sharp regional sell off on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Japan's benchmark Nikkei average opened firmer on after the country's first quarter growth figures came in stronger than expected.   \par \par \uc2 Taiwan shares also opened slightly firmer on Tuesday after a sharp drop on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Investors will be on the look out for further signs that funds are moving out of the Asia region.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market late on Monday afternoon in order to maintain the Hong Kong dollar peg to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders say funds are moving money back into U.S. dollar-denominated money market funds in anticipation of a U.S. rate rise as early as next month with funds squaring positions in Hong Kong blue chips and China plays after solid gains.   \par \par \uc2 Investors will also watching for further macroeconomic measures to cool China's surging economy.   \par \par \uc2 China will continue to control bank loans and money supply, and actively studying means, such as interest rate hikes, to rein its economic growth, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy committee member Li Yang said on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Li said if consumer prices moved above five percent the government may raise interest rates, though under current conditions he believed China would not raise rates in the next six months.   \par \par \uc2 China shares traded in New York were hammered overnight.   \par \par \uc2 Asia's largest oil refiner Sinopec Corp (0386.HK) (SNP.N) fell 8.19 percent to US$32.08, while China's largest oil producer PetroChina Co Ltd (0857.HK) (PTR.N) lost 5.71 percent to US$41.63.   \par \par \uc2 China Eastern Airlines Ltd (CEA.N) (0670.HK) fell 5.14 percent to US$16.79.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040518e05i0002p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
