\par\par \b \uc2 HK stocks knocked by China overheating, high oil prices.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tara Joseph   \par \uc2 684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2004\par 10:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, May 17 (Reuters) - Leading Hong Kong shares ended sharply lower on Monday, tapping fresh year lows as investors worried that China's economy may be overheating and global oil prices hit new record highs.   \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shadowed other regional stocks markets which were all bruised by a rising U.S. dollar and a highly anticipated interest rate hike in the U.S. next month, encouraging funds flows out of Asia in favour of dollar-denominated assets.   \par \par \uc2 "These factors are all negative for the region and unfortunately they are all happening at the same time," said Aaron Pong, investment director at Standard Life Investments.   \par \par \uc2 The benchmark Hang Seng Index ended down 2.74 percent, or 309.21 points, at 10,967.65.   \par \par \uc2 Volume was above recent averages with HK$16.13 billion (US$2.06 billion) changing hands.   \par \par \uc2 Shares in China registered companies listed in Hong Kong were particularly hard hit with commodity stocks such as Yanzhou Coal (1171.HK), Asia oil refining giant Sinopec Corp (0386.HK) (SNP.N) and top Chinese oil producer PetroChina (0857.HK) (PTR.N) all falling by more than five percent.   \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong blue-chip property and interest-rate sensitive financial stocks such as Sun Hung Kai Properties (0016.HK) and the Bank of China's Hong Kong arm, BOC Hong Kong (Holdings) Ltd (2388.HK), also tumbled. Sun Hung Kai fell 5.3 percent and BOCHK dropped nearly seven percent.   \par \par \uc2 FUNDS LEAVING ASIA   \par \par \uc2 Crude oil hit a new all-time high of US$41.64 a barrel in electronic trade on Monday, fuelling fresh inflation fears and sending stocks lower across Asia.   \par \par \uc2 Traders also cited fresh evidence of international funds selling Asia stocks in favour of U.S. money market assets.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market late on Monday afternoon in order to maintain the Hong Kong dollar peg to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "Right now a lot of regional funds are trying to clean up their portfolio. They are aggressively removing second-tier stocks," said Herbert Lau, research director of Celestial Asia Securities.   \par \par \uc2 Investors also cited frustration with the lack of direction on China's plans to cool its economy.   \par \par \uc2 Chinese Vice Finance Minister Li Yong on Monday warned against applying the brakes too strongly on his country's racing economy.   \par \par \uc2 Li, who oversees financial reform, repeated the government's target for seven percent growth this year, but did not say how the country would get there.   \par \par \uc2 "Authorities continue to focus on administrative policy and I think this gives investors the feeling that the policy plan is still being adjusted on a day-to-day basis," Pong said.   \par \par \uc2 Chinese authorities have restricted lending to some industries and told local officials to forego new development zones for manufacturing.   \par \par \uc2 But some investors believe Beijing will eventually move to raise lending rates in China for the first time in nine years.   \par \par \uc2 The H-share index of China registered companies listed in Hong Kong fell 5.36 percent to 3,546.25.   \par \par \uc2 PROPERTY SUFFERS   \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong property stocks took a further battering on signs that activity in the city's property sector is cooling after at least six months of strong buying activity.   \par \par \uc2 The Hang Seng properties sub index fell 4.76 percent to 12,809.35.   \par \par \uc2 Shares in global bank global bank HSBC Holdings (0005.HK) fell 0.9 percent to HK$109.50 after three small bombs exploded outside branches of HSBC branches in Turkey on Sunday ahead of a visit by British Prime Minister Tony Blair. HSBC makes up one-third of the weighting of the benchmark Hang Seng Index.   \par \par \uc2 Free to air broadcaster Television Broadcasts Ltd (TVB) (0511.HK) was the biggest blue chip loser of the day, sliding nearly 12 percent following media reports over the weekend that the firm was suffering further delays in its bid to secure broadcasting rights in mainland China.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said market was looking oversold with the 10,800 level expected to provide some support for the Hang Seng on Tuesday.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040517e05h000x9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
