\par\par \b \uc2 HK stocks seen range trading, fund flow worries persist.   \b0\par\par\uc2 480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2004\par 03:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, May 13 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks are expected to track sideways on Thursday with investors sharing global concerns over high oil prices and escalating Middle East violence while eyeing fund flows out of the territory.   \par \par \uc2 But badly bruised China plays may get a boost from the latest measures announced to tame China's sizzling economy, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said late on Wednesday that it will expand open-market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  from once to twice-weekly.   \par \par \uc2 Investors are on alert for a raft of possible measures China may implement to cool economic growth of 9.7 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Speaking in Dublin on Wednesday, Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao expressed concern about the pace of investment growth in some sectors of the economy as well as the over-expansion in money supply.   \par \par \uc2 The blue chip Hang Seng Index gained a slim 0.17 percent on Wednesday, closing at 11,528.18.   \par \par \uc2 Investors will also be on watch for further signs that funds are leaving \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the city's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly on Wednesday to maintain the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar peg to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "That will continue to make investors a little worried. We need some positive news." said Ben Kwong, associate director at KGI Asia Ltd.   \par \par \uc2 Wall Street was mixed with bargain hunting setting in despite persistent worries about skyrocketing oil prices, rising interest rates and violence in Iraq. The blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average ended up 0.26 percent at 10,045.16, after tumbling by nearly 1.5 percent by midday. The tech heavy Nasdaq Composite Index fell 0.30 percent to 1,925.59.   \par \par \uc2 Oil prices settled in New York at their highest level ever at US$40.77 a barrel due to worries over escalating violence in the Middle East and a worries about inventory levels of gasoline heading into the summer driving season.   \par \par \uc2 China oil stocks traded in New York, however, put in a mixed performance.   \par \par \uc2 China's largest oil producer PetroChina Co Ltd (0857.HK) (PTR.N) fell 0.84 percent to US$44.67.   \par \par \uc2 Asia's largest oil refiner Sinopec Corp (SNP.N) (0386.HK) rose 1.01 percent to US$34.90.   \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's Cathay Pacific Airways Ltd (0293.HK), the world's third largest airline by market value, said on Wednesday it was considering a fuel surcharge on ticket prices because of surging oil prices.   \par \par \uc2 "I'd say strong oil prices are here for the balance of the year," Cathay Chairman James Hughes-Hallett told reporters after the airline's annual general meeting.   \par \par \uc2 "It (a fuel surcharge) is under active review at the moment," he added.   \par \par \uc2 Hughes-Hallett said that should jet fuel prices remain high it would affect the airline in an "unfavourable way", adding that fuel accounts for 20 percent of operating costs.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040513e05d0006k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
