\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Stocks End Mixed; Techs, Car Makers Sink Tokyo  \b0\par\par\uc2 783 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 2004\par 13:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Wall Street Journal Online News Roundup   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares across the Asian-Pacific region closed mixed Monday. Concerns over the weakness of the dollar hurt tech stocks and car makers in Tokyo. Hong Kong and China markets rose on expectations of good earnings reports.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Tokyo's benchmark stock index sank to a six-week low, as the dollar's slide against the yen and the approaching March 31 fiscal-year end prompted investors to sell technology and exporter blue chips.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei Stock Average of 225 issues closed down 58.31 points, or 0.6%, at 10402.61, after having risen more than 100 points earlier.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was trading at 105.55 yen in Tokyo late Monday, down 49 yen from late Friday in Tokyo and also below the 105.91 yen it bought in New York later that day.  \par \par \uc2 Stocks were initially buoyed by signs that Tokyo had won concessions from G-7 nations at their weekend meeting to halt the yen's surge.  \par \par \uc2 Technology blue chips NTT DoCoMo and Fujitsu, auto makers Toyota Motor and Honda Motor. Major banks were mostly lower. UFJ Holdings slipped 0.2% and Mizuho Financial lost 1.7%.  \par \par \uc2 Sony added 1.2% and Canon edged up 0.4%.  \par \par \uc2 In a joint statement at the end of a two-day meeting in Boca Raton, Florida, G-7 finance officials said they support "more flexibility" in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but also noted concerns about "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates." The statement appeared to both endorse U.S. dollar's recent dive against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  while warning that Japan and European countries might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold the dollar steady.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  shares advanced Monday as hopes of upbeat earnings news from blue chips drove the key index sharply higher for the second consecutive session.  \par \par \uc2 The blue-chip Hang Seng Index ended up 267.08 points, or 2%, at 13576.68. The broader S&P/HKEx LargeCap finished ahead 313.29 points at 14945.28.  \par \par \uc2 "Investors are expecting good news from blue chips. Improved earnings can also help to lower the price-to-earnings ratio of Hong Kong shares," said Mark Wan, head of research at Tanrich Financial Group.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts are expecting much-improved earnings for Hong Kong companies on a recovering domestic economy. Blue chips Bank of East Asia and Esprit Holdings will kick off the reporting season this week. Bank of East Asia climbed 1.8%. Most of its banking peers also gained. HSBC Holdings rose 1.6%, Hang Seng Bank rose 1.4%, and BOC Hong Kong finished 2.8% higher. Fashion retail Esprit Holdings rose 3.6%, lifted by expectation a strong euro will continue to help its Europe-based \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 All but one of the 33 blue chips rose, with laggards producing the strongest gains. CITIC Pacific jumped 6%, while fixed-line telecom operator PCCW rose 4.1%.  \par \par \uc2 China's shares ended higher, with the benchmark Shanghai Composite Index closing above 1700 for the first time in eighteen months on hopes more foreign money could flow into the stock market.  \par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Securities News reported Monday that so-called qualified foreign institutional investors were looking to increase their investments in the stock market, and that UBS may ask for its investment quota in China to be raised. UBS declined to comment.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index, which tracks both A and B shares, rose 1.5% to close at 1703.53. It was the highest level for the index since July 19, 2002. The Shenzhen Composite Index gained 2.1% to end at 435.75.  \par \par \uc2 Retail stocks led the gainers after Nanjing Xinjiekou Department Store said a company called Huamei became its second largest shareholder by buying shares in the secondary market. Analysts said Huamei, also based in Nanjing, could help restructure Xinjiekou, giving investors hope other retailers could be restructured in the future.  \par \par \uc2 Shanghai No.1 Department Store rose by the 10% daily limit, Shanghai Hualian also rose 10%, and Nanjing Xinjiekou Department Store rose 2.3%.  \par \par \uc2 The Australian share market closed 0.5% higher, supported by gains on Wall Street and a quarterly statement from the Reserve Bank of Australia. The star performers were market heavyweight News Corp. and resource and banking stocks.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark S&P/ASX 200 closed 14.9 points higher at 3287.1.  \par \par \uc2 The Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quarterly statement on monetary policy Monday painted a positive picture of domestic and global growth. News Corp., which generates 77% of its revenue in the U.S., rose 1.2% although volume was light ahead of the media group's second quarter and first half results due Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020040209e0290000c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
