\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-HK dlr peg architect defends it, sees no change.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan   \par \uc2 654 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2004\par 09:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 16 (Reuters) - John Greenwood, the economist credited with designing the Hong Kong dollar peg, on Friday rejected criticism that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had caused the city's economic troubles and said he saw no need to change the system.    \par \par \uc2  "Fundamentally, there is no need for the HK dollar peg to be revalued or devalued," said Greenwood, the chief economist of London-based INVESCO Asset Management Ltd and a member of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board.    \par \par \uc2  "The desire to repeg represents a misunderstanding of the way in which the mechanism is designed to operate," he told Reuters.    \par \par \uc2  In 1983 the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  adopted Greenwood's suggestion that the Hong Kong dollar's  value be fixed against the U.S. dollar.    \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar rate was steady near 7.75 until 1999, and then near 7.8 per U.S. dollar until September 2003, when speculation on a revaluation pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above 7.7 per dollar.    \par \par \uc2  Prices for the Hong Kong dollar in the forward market  imply further appreciation.    \par \par \uc2  Greenwood dismissed the idea that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  needed a lower peg or to trade freely, either to resolve economic problems or to mimick any revaluation of the Chinese yuan .    \par \par \uc2  Also, undesirable political interference tended to come with the different types of managed exchange rate bands that other Asian economies had adopted, he said.    \par \par \uc2  "The Singapore way has been supported in the past by some degree of controls either on balance sheets or directing of savings or restrictions on certain types of capital and derivative transactions," Greenwood said.    \par \par \uc2  "Hong Kong has never had those sort of controls and Hong Kong does not want to go down that route. The background is different and the objective is to keep markets very open and very liquid."    \par \par \uc2  THE ECONOMY SWAYS    \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's economy and prices had in the past made the adjustments needed to keep the peg steady, he said.    \par \par \uc2  "The mechanism is very strong. There is no reason why the Hong Kong dollar should move if the renminbi is changed."    \par \par \uc2  The peg has been regarded as the pillar of the Hong Kong economy, the world's 27th largest in 2002 if counted separately from China's, and as a necessity for its open, service-based structure. But critics blame the peg for the territory's five years of deflation and high joblessness.    \par \par \uc2  Greenwood defends his brainchild fiercely. Circumstances have been extraordinarily harsh, he says.    \par \par \uc2  "The system wasn't really designed to take into account 40 to 50 percent devaluations of its neighbours," he said, referring to Asia's financial crisis in 1997/98.    \par \par \uc2  "And the dramatic opening of China would have required very significant (economic) adjustments in any case. The fact that those two things came together has intensified the difficulty of the past five-six years."    \par \par \uc2  LONG LIFE    \par \par \uc2  Still, Greenwood said, the Hong Kong peg had been a success until the crisis and there was no reason it could not salvage that reputation.    \par \par \uc2  Besides, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  was pumping cash into the economy by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, buying US$7 billion in the past four months to push Hong Kong dollar back down towards 7.8 per dollar.    \par \par \uc2  This would drive interest rates lower, encourage borrowing and stimulate greater activity, he said.    \par \par \uc2  Greeenwood said the Hong Kong dollar need not always be anchored to the U.S. dollar and that the yuan might make a good choice when it became convertible.    \par \par \uc2  But the peg would stay.    \par \par \uc2  "I was criticised initially for saying this system will be around for many decades," Greenwood said.    \par \par \uc2  "It has lasted for two decades. As long as the economy is flexible and can adjust to the exchange rate, it doesn't really matter whether the economy is in the era of sailing ships, battle ships or space ships."    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040116e01g001k6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
