\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Jan 13  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,806 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2004\par 10:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Europe has now joined Japan in publicly fretting about the dollar's fall, but the lack of a global consensus means central bankers and finance ministers are unlikely to agree in coming weeks on any message to stem the dollar's weakness. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia:G7 Likely Inconclusive On \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Despite ECB Concern" ran at 0517 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's money supply growth was at its slowest rate in a decade and bank lending slumped 5.0% on year in the October-December quarter, adding to expectations the Bank of Japan will keep its ultra-loose monetary policy at least through 2004. ("=WRAP: Japan Money Growth Slows, Encourages Easy Policy" ran at 0720 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's economic growth accelerated to a six-year high of 8.5% in 2003, a senior government official revealed Tuesday, highlighting the economy's sharp rebound from last year's SARS epidemic. ("=WRAP: China's 2003 GDP Hits 6-Yr High, Drives Tax Growth" ran at 0841 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: As investors continue to pour money into Hong Kong stocks and properties, the combination of that influx of funds with the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg is causing some pretty strange things to happen in local financial markets. ("=Hong Kong Liquidity Surge Creates Oddity In Bond Market" ran at 0534 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold HK$737 million Tuesday, buying about US$95 million, as it continued its regular market activities to stabilize the Hong Kong dollar's exchange rate. The HKMA also sold HK$4.17 billion in similar \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday. Including Tuesday's sale, it has sold nearly HK$47 billion into the market since late September, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's strength first prompted the moves. ("HKMA/\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -2: Buys About US$95 Million" ran at 0746 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: China and Malaysia will continue to peg their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. dollar in the near term, Bank of America said Tuesday, citing Beijing's need to first build up its financial infrastructure, and cautioned on Kuala Lumpur ahead of expected national elections this year. (story will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Deutsche Bank predicts South Korea's economy will likely grow 5% in 2004, but deems the government's 6.5% growth target feasible, said a senior economist at the investment bank. ("Deutsche Bk/S Korea Econ -2: Econ Growth Likely 5% '04" first take ran at 0259 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's latest "Buy American" trade delegation is expected to draw officials from Motorola and Lucent Technologies to a contract-signing ceremony Tuesday in Washington D.C. (story will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: The China Banking Regulatory Commission said Tuesday it will issue quarterly nonperforming loan data for the country's bank sector using a more reliable calculation method. ("China Bk Regulator To Issue More Reliable,Timely NPL Data" ran at 0751 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: A proposal to establish a national greenhouse emissions trading system in Australia is feasible but policy-makers should forget about expanding the market overseas, said leading Australian academic and Reserve Bank of Australia board member Warwick McKibbin. ("Australia RBA Bd Member McKibbin Backs Emissions Scheme" ran at 0112 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thai Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra said Tuesday the current baht level is appropriate as it reflects the country's robust economic conditions. The baht hit a 3.5-year high against the dollar Monday amid general bearish sentiment for the U.S. unit. ("Thai PM/Baht -2: Baht Stability Is Prime Concern" ran at 0238 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HANOI: Vietnam's Ministry of Transportation is planning to raise trillions of dong in the next few years by selling bonds to finance the country's road construction projects, a senior ministry official said Tuesday. ("Vietnam Plans More Bonds To Finance VND110T Road Projects" ran at 0650 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =INSIDE INDOCHINA: Vietnam is gearing up for another trade spat with the U.S. - this time over shrimp. Barely six months after the U.S. ruled that Vietnam was dumping catfish in the country, its shrimp farmers are charging that shrimp imports from Vietnam are being sold in the U.S. at below market price. ("=INSIDE INDOCHINA:Vietnam To Bear Brunt Of US Shrimp Suit" ran at 0318 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The euro rose against the dollar in Asia Tuesday, quickly recovering from initial weakness following European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet's expression of concern Monday about the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent fast-paced ascent. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Euro Up Vs Dollar As Trichet Impact Fades" ran at 0531 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Samsung Electronics, the world's largest memory chip maker, Tuesday announced plans to invest a total 3.9 trillion won to boost its output of liquid crystal displays, flash memory and dynamic random access memory (DRAM) chips. The company also named Hwang Chang-Gyu as president of its semiconductor \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("Samsung Elec/CEO -2: Yoon Jong Yong To Remain Vice-Chmn" ran at 0301 GMT; "Samsung Elec/Invest-2: To Boost DRAM, Flash Memory Output" ran at 0642 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Samsung Electronics will likely report Thursday its fourth-quarter net profit rose nearly 40% from a year ago, helped by surging demand for flash memory chips and liquid crystal displays used in flat-screen televisions and notebook computers. ("=Samsung Elec's 4Q Net Likely Rose On Flash Memory, LCDs" ran at 0345 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Hewlett-Packard said Tuesday it will relocate some of its manufacturing from the U.S. to Singapore and spend more than US$1 billion over five years to upgrade its Southeast Asian manufacturing hub in the city-state. The sizable investment will enable the world's No. 2 computer maker to use Singapore as a base to meet growing demand from China and South Korea for products such as high-end servers and printers. ("=WRAP: Hewlett-Packard To Invest US$1 Bln In Singapore Mfg" ran at 0639 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Shares in Chinese oil giants took a hit Tuesday after BP PLC said it was selling its 2% stake in PetroChina Co., prompting worries of more divestments by oil majors in other mainland oil firms. However, a fund manager at a U.S. house said resilient oil prices and global geopolitical tensions would support shares of mainland oil companies. ("=WRAP: Chinese Oil Shares Slump On BP's PetroChina Exit" ran at 0844 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China Green, a Fujian province-based farming and food processing company, ended 58% higher on its first trading day on the Hong Kong stock exchange's main board, riding a wave of investor enthusiasm for new China listings. The shares closed at HK$2.025, well above the session low of HK$1.80 and a little below the day high of HK$2.55. The retail tranche was oversubscribed 1,603 times, making it Hong Kong's most popular ever IPO. ("China Green/Debut -2: Third Most Actively Traded By Vol" ran at 0207 GMT; WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: National Australia Bank warned Tuesday that unauthorized \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  options trades may erase up to A$180 million from its pretax profit, which prompted a rally by the Australian dollar as the bank scrambled to cover its losses, dealers said. ("Natl Australia Bk/Trading Losses" ran at 0250 GMT; WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: State-run Korea Development Bank plans to complete the sale of a controlling stake in LG Investment & Securities by end-May as part of a bailout package for LG Card, according to an outline released by the bank Tuesday. ("Korea Devt Bk/LG Card -2: To Close Brokerage Deal In May" ran at 0657 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: Reliance Industries' telecom unit completes its $211 million purchase of U.S.-based Flag Telecom, the biggest acquisition ever by an Indian company. (WRAP will run around 1130 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: The Australian Securities and Investments Commission confirmed Tuesday it is questioning the country's fund managers in the wake of the mutual fund scandal in the U.S. ("Australia's ASIC Questions Funds Following US Scandal" ran at 0203 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Okasan Securities said Tuesday it has started selling an investment trust that includes some class A shares listed in China, making it the first Japanese broker to sell such a product. ("Okasan Sec To Offer Trust Investing In China Class A Shrs" ran at 0318 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Personal computer buyers are showing an increasing preference for notebook-style PCs and Taiwan's cost-conscious notebook computer makers look set to ride that trend to higher sales - and profits - in 2004. ("=Notebook PC Boom A Boon For Taiwan's Contract PC Makers," ran at 0806 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: CapitaLand Ltd., Southeast-Asia's largest listed property developer, Tuesday said it plans to explore opportunities in India to boost overseas profits amid a saturated home market, sending its share price up 7% at the close. ("Singapore CapitaLand Plans To Explore Indian Market" ran at 0746 GMT; WRAP will run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Defense contractor Singapore Technologies Engineering's flat earnings growth due to a slump in the airline industry has capped its share price in the past two years, but its optimistic outlook has now prompted "buy" calls. ("=Asian Stock Focus: ST Engineering Shrs To Power Ahead" ran at 0400 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Inner Mongolian BaoTou Steel Union Co. said Tuesday its board reapproved plans to issue 1.8 billion yuan in convertible bonds to finance a cold-rolled steel project. ("China BaoTou Steel Plans CNY1.8B Conv Bond For Steel Proj" ran at 0259 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA FUND VIEW: Even if the current power struggle between Sri Lanka's leaders is resolved soon, the ill effects will continue to be felt on the domestic economy and share prices for months to come, said Rachini Rajapaksa, a senior fund manager at Unit Trust Management Co. (=Asia Fund View will run around 0945 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Japanese oil refiners may be forced to further reduce their planned operating rates in January and February due to weak domestic refining margins for several oil products, traders said Tuesday. ("=Weak Japanese Oil Demand May Force Refiners To Cut Rates" ran at 0743 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's Resolute Mining will become the country's third-biggest gold producer after it agreed Tuesday to acquire Swiss-based Xstrata's Ravenswood gold mine in Queensland state for US$45 million in cash. ("Australia's Resolute Buys Xstrata Gold Mine In Queenland" ran at 0350 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 62550  \p