\par\par \b \uc2 WRAP: HKMA Sells HK$1.59 Bln In Tuesday  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 329 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2004\par 13:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a total of HK$1.59 billion, buying about US$205 million, during local business hours Tuesday, as it continued its regular market activities aimed at stabilizing the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 Since the Hong Kong dollar first strengthened beyond its peg rate of HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar in late September, the HKMA has sold more than HK$50 billion in the markets, including Tuesday's transactions, to stem the local unit's rise. The sales are now happening nearly every day: the HKMA sold HK$4.17 billion Monday in similar transactions.  \par \par \uc2 After the last of Tuesday's sales, the U.S. dollar was quoted around HK$7.7630, still within its recent tight range. Traders said the HKMA appears to be selling Hong Kong dollars to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from moving any higher than that level.  \par \par \uc2 But since the HKMA's sales are just offsetting large purchases of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by investors interested in the rising local stock and property markets, they are mainly preventing swings in the exchange rate rather than pushing it back toward the peg.  \par \par \uc2 A return to the peg rate is unlikely until the strong fund inflows to Hong Kong reverse, traders say. And with investor interest still strong, that isn't expected to happen any time soon.  \par \par \uc2 Though its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged to the U.S. dollar, Hong Kong's system doesn't prevent the local unit from strengthening if, as at present, there are large and continuing flows of funds into Hong Kong dollar assets.  \par \par \uc2 The main consequence of the HKMA's sales to ease the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength has been a surge in interbank liquidity, which is pushing down local interest rates to historical lows near zero.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Andrew Bullard  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040113e01d000i0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
