\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA Sells Another HK$854 Mln In Day's 2nd  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 314 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2004\par 09:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold another HK$854 million, buying about US$110 million, in its second \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday to stabilize the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The latest sale brings the day's total to HK$1.24 billion, which would buy about US$160 million. But the additional injection of funds didn't have any immediate effect on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot and forward markets.  \par \par \uc2 After the sale, the U.S. dollar was quoted at HK$7.7628, still within its recent tight range around HK$7.7630. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders expect the HKMA to continue to sell Hong Kong dollars as needed to prevent the local unit from strengthening beyond that level.  \par \par \uc2 Since the Hong Kong dollar first strengthened beyond its peg rate of HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar in late September, the HKMA has sold HK$46 billion in the markets, including Monday's transactions, to ease the local unit's rise.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the HKMA's sales of Hong Kong dollars are primarily offsetting large purchases of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by investors seeking to profit from the recent strong performance of local stocks and rising Hong Kong property prices. Some buyers of Hong Kong dollars are also speculating on the possible appreciation of the Chinese yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Though the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged to the U.S. dollar, the system doesn't prevent the local unit from strengthening if, as at present, there are large and continuing flows of funds into Hong Kong dollar assets.  \par \par \uc2 While the HKMA's sales can help prevent dramatic swings in the exchange rate in this situation, a return to the peg rate is unlikely until the strong fund inflows reverse, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Andrew Bullard  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040112e01c000fd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
