\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA Sells HK$388 Mln In Latest Move To Steady HK Dollar  \b0\par\par\uc2 311 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2004\par 07:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold HK$388 million Monday afternoon, buying about US$50 million, in the latest of its increasingly regular \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar from strengthening.  \par \par \uc2 After the sale, the U.S. dollar was quoted at HK$7.7628, still within its recent tight range around HK$7.7630. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders expect the HKMA to continue to sell Hong Kong dollars as needed to prevent the local unit from strengthening beyond that level.  \par \par \uc2 The discount on Hong Kong dollar forwards widened, however, to 760-740 below the spot rate, from 745-715 earlier in the day, reflecting the additional interbank liquidity brought about by the sale.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the HKMA's sales of Hong Kong dollars are primarily offsetting large purchases of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by investors seeking to profit from the recent strong performance of local stocks and rising Hong Kong property prices. Some buyers of Hong Kong dollars are also speculating on the possible appreciation of the Chinese yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Though the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged at HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar, the system doesn't prevent the local unit from strengthening if, as at present, there are large and continuing flows of funds into Hong Kong dollar assets.  \par \par \uc2 Though the HKMA's sales can help prevent dramatic swings in the exchange rate in this situation, a return to the peg rate is unlikely until the strong fund inflows reverse, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Since the Hong Kong dollar first strengthened beyond the peg rate in late September, the HKMA has sold more than HK$45 billion in the markets, including Monday's transaction, to ease the local unit's rise.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Andrew Bullard  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040112e01c000a0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
