\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-HSBC cuts HK savings rate amid liquidity flood.   \b0\par\par\uc2 299 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2004\par 10:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Jan 9 (Reuters) - HSBC Holdings Plc (0005.HK) (HSBA.L) said on Friday it would cut its savings rate in Hong Kong for local dollar deposits to 0.001 percent from 0.01 percent, a move analysts say is prompted by a flood of liquidity.   \par \par \uc2 The bank said the rate applies to accounts with balances of HK$10,000 (US$1,282) or more, effective on Monday. Depositors with less than that will get nothing.   \par \par \uc2 HSBC's Hong Kong unit said in a statement that it would not change its best lending rate, currently 5.0 percent. Bank spokespeople were not immediately available for comment.   \par \par \uc2 "I think it (the unexpected HSBC cut) mainly reflects high liquidity," said Tony Liu, an analyst at DBS Securities.   \par \par \uc2 "The HIBOR rate has been very weak in the past quarter," he said, referring to the Hong Kong interbank offered rate.   \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's banking system is awash in money as foreign investors pile into its surging stock market and park cash here for investments in booming mainland China.   \par \par \uc2 The territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in money markets in recent months, selling \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars in a bid to curb growing spot market strength in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is pegged to the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold HK$11.45 billion so far this week alone, bringing the aggregate balance - a key measure of interbank liquidity - to nearly HK$44 billion.   \par \par \uc2 Liu said that HSBC traditionally had the lowest savings rates of Hong Kong banks given its dominant market position. Other banks may not wish to follow suit to keep customers, he added. (US$=HK$7.8)   \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Katie Hunt and Brian Kelleher).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040109e019001sx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
