\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA Sells Another HK$1.01 Bln In Latest  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 332 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2004\par 09:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  again sold Hong Kong dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Tuesday, in its latest effort to prevent the local unit from strengthening.  \par \par \uc2 The sale of HK$1.01 billion in the afternoon, after a sale of HK$776 million in the morning, brought the day's total sales to HK$1.79 billion, buying about US$230 million.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the HKMA's sales were intended to offset the impact on the exchange rate from inflows of money into Hong Kong, as investors look to invest in the local stock market or speculate on a possible appreciation in the Chinese yuan.  \par \par \uc2 The spot exchange rate of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unmoved following the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The U.S. dollar was quoted at HK$7.7628. However, the discount on Hong Kong dollar forwards deepened, to 700 to 680 below the spot rate, from 685 to 675 earlier.  \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system automatically prevents the local unit from weakening away from the peg rate of HK$7.80 to the U.S. dollar, but there is no such provision in the system to prevent it from strengthening - only the HKMA's discretion to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in individual transactions.  \par \par \uc2 Since late September, the Hong Kong dollar has been trading at a rate stronger than its official peg, an unusual situation driven by heavy flows of foreign investment into Hong Kong assets.  \par \par \uc2 During that time, the HKMA has sold more than HK$32 billion in its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Its efforts have, however, managed only to cap the rise of the local unit at a level of around HK$7.76. With continued inflows of funds into Hong Kong, the sales haven't been enough to push it back to its usual narrow range around the peg rate.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Andrew Bullard  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040106e016000a4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
